#!/bin/bash
# Shadows the real gamescope binary via PATH (see
# gamescope-session.service.d/override.conf's PATH= override) --
# /usr/lib/steamos/gamescope-session's own `exec gamescope \ ...` line is
# hardcoded with no env-var-driven way to append extra flags, so this is
# the only way to inject --expose-wayland without patching that packaged
# script directly (which would get overwritten on the next
# gamescope-session-cachyos update).
#
# --expose-wayland: confirmed via the ArchWiki Gamescope page -- "Gamescope
# does not support Wayland clients by default." Without this flag, a game
# launched with PROTON_ENABLE_WAYLAND=1 has nowhere to actually connect --
# its Wayland surface silently never gets created, which is exactly why
# that experiment hung forever on Steam's "waiting for game" spinner rather
# than showing an error. Harmless to keep even with PROTON_ENABLE_WAYLAND
# unset -- XWayland-hosted games are unaffected either way.
#
# --hdr-enabled: turns on gamescope's HDR output path at startup. Strictly
# speaking optional -- the packaged session passes STEAM_GAMESCOPE_HDR_
# SUPPORTED=1 and Steam flips HDR on at runtime over the gamescope control
# protocol, so HDR worked here before this wrapper existed. Kept because it
# makes the output's HDR state explicit and independent of Steam's setting
# rather than something that has to be re-toggled in the UI.
#
# --hdr-itm-enable: inverse tone mapping, i.e. expand *SDR* content to fill
# the HDR range. It does not touch genuinely HDR titles (those carry their
# own colorspace and metadata through the WSI layer). Purely taste -- drop
# it if SDR games look over-lifted.
#
# Note neither HDR flag was the fix for the long "washed out HDR" hunt.
# That was a leaked WAYLAND_DISPLAY disabling gamescope's WSI layer inside
# the game's pressure-vessel container, so gamescope never learned the
# app's real colorspace and treated HDR content as sRGB. See
# config/systemd/README.md and steam-launcher.service.d/override.conf.
exec /usr/bin/gamescope --expose-wayland --hdr-enabled --hdr-itm-enable "$@"
